(window.DOM_CHAPTERS=window.DOM_CHAPTERS||{})["ecg"]="<h1>Electrocardiography and Basic Rhythms</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-what-does-an-ecg-actually-record\"><span class=\"sec-num\">1.</span> What does an ECG actually record?</h2>\n<p>Each heartbeat starts with an electrical signal that spreads through the heart muscle. As the muscle cells depolarise (switch on) and then repolarise (reset), they create small voltage differences. These spread through the body's tissues, all the way to the skin. Electrodes on the skin pick them up, and the machine draws them over time. That drawing is the <strong>electrocardiogram (ECG, or EKG)</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"ecg-concept-line\" aria-label=\"Electrical and mechanical sequence\"><span>Electrical activation</span><b>→</b><span>Contraction</span><b>→</b><span>Pressure changes</span><b>→</b><span>Blood movement</span></div>\n\n<p><strong>An ECG records electricity, not pumping.</strong> It does not measure how strongly the heart contracts, the pressure it makes or how much blood it ejects. A <span data-term=\"qrs\">QRS complex</span> means the ventricles have been <strong>switched on</strong> electrically, not that they have pumped blood. Usually contraction follows. But the electrical signal can carry on even when the heart muscle pumps little or no blood. So a normal-looking ECG does not prove the heart is pumping well.</p>\n<div class=\"media-resource video-resource\" data-youtube-id=\"HQqPYH7pdPM\" data-media-title=\"(ECG or EKG) Rhythm Strips part 1 — BioVFX\"><span class=\"media-eyebrow\">OPTIONAL VIDEO · BIOVFX ECG SERIES, PART 1 OF 3</span><h3>(ECG or EKG) Rhythm Strips part 1</h3><p>BioVFX. An introduction to ECG recordings, ECG paper and rhythm strips, and how to measure time using the boxes on the paper. It loads only when tapped.</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"media-play\" data-media-action=\"play-video\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Play video</button> <a class=\"media-outlink\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HQqPYH7pdPM\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open on YouTube</a><div class=\"media-stage\"></div><p class=\"media-credit\">© BioVFX, shown via YouTube's privacy-enhanced embed. The written explanation on this page does not depend on this video.</p></div>\n\n<h2 id=\"2-why-can-the-tracing-move-upwards-or-downwards\"><span class=\"sec-num\">2.</span> Why can the tracing move upwards or downwards?</h2>\n<p>First, two words that are easy to mix up:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>An <strong>electrode</strong> is the sticker on the skin.</li>\n<li>A <strong>lead</strong> is one view of the heart's electrical activity, worked out from the voltage between electrodes.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Think of each lead as a camera</strong> pointed at the heart from one direction. Every lead watches the same heartbeat, but from a different angle, so each draws a slightly different shape.</p>\n<p>At any moment, the electrical wave spreading through the heart has an overall <strong>direction</strong> and <strong>size</strong>. We draw it as an arrow, the electrical <strong>vector</strong>. What a lead records depends on which way that arrow points compared with the lead:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>The wave of depolarisation travels…</th>\n  <th>The lead draws…</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Towards</strong> the lead's positive end</td>\n  <td>An upward deflection</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Away from</strong> it</td>\n  <td>A downward deflection</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Across</strong> it, at right angles</td>\n  <td>A small or up-and-down (biphasic) deflection</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Example:</strong> in a normal heart, the ventricles depolarise mostly downwards and to the patient's left. Lead II looks at the heart from that direction, so its QRS is usually upright. aVR looks from the right shoulder, so the same wave travels away from it, and its QRS points down.</p>\n<p><strong>The up and down are electrical, not movement.</strong> An upward wave does not mean the heart moved up. It means the electrical wave was heading towards that lead.</p>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure teaching-widget\" data-widget=\"lead-view\" data-desc=\"sr\"><figcaption>Lead view</figcaption><p>A heart outline with one lead's positive end marked. Drag or tap to turn the depolarisation arrow. A small trace beside it updates live: upward when the arrow points towards the lead, downward when it points away, and a small up-and-down deflection when it points across. Buttons switch between Lead I, Lead II and aVR. Default: Lead II with the normal ventricular direction (upright QRS).</p></figure>\n\n<p><strong>Why is the normal <span data-term=\"t-wave\">T wave</span> upright, if it is &quot;the opposite&quot; of depolarisation?</strong> The rule in the table is for depolarisation. Repolarisation (the cells resetting) gives the opposite sign: a resetting wave travelling <em>towards</em> a lead draws a <em>downward</em> deflection. But in the ventricles, the last cells to switch on (the outer layer) are the first to reset. So repolarisation travels in the <strong>reverse direction</strong>, from outside to inside. Opposite sign plus opposite direction cancel out, so the T wave usually points the same way as the QRS.</p>\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> The lecture does not explain T-wave direction. The outer-layer-first order of ventricular repolarisation is standard textbook physiology (Guyton and Hall).</aside>\n\n<section class=\"check-block\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"check-launch\" aria-haspopup=\"dialog\" data-questions=\"3\" data-part=\"Sections 1–2\"><span class=\"cl-badge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">?</span><span class=\"cl-text\"><strong>Understanding Check</strong><span class=\"cl-sub\">Sections 1–2 · 3 questions</span></span><span class=\"cl-go\">Start</span></button><div class=\"check-body\"><p class=\"check-label\"><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 1–2</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> What is the difference between an ECG electrode and an ECG lead?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. An electrode is a skin contact; a lead is a view worked out from electrodes</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. An electrode records voltage; a lead records the strength of contraction</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. An electrode is placed on the chest; a lead is always placed on a limb</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. An electrode is one view of the heart; a lead is the wire to the machine</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"ecg:7291ca0b\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> Electrodes are the physical contacts. Leads are views calculated from the voltages between them, so ten electrodes can give twelve leads.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> In one lead, the wave of ventricular depolarisation travels away from the positive end. What does that lead draw?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. An upward QRS, because the ventricles are contracting</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. A downward QRS, because the wave is moving away</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. A flat line, because no wave is heading towards it</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. A taller T wave, because repolarisation comes first</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"ecg:0b1c764e\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> Depolarisation moving towards a lead draws an upward deflection, and moving away draws a downward one. The deflection shows direction, not contraction.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> A patient's ECG shows regular, normal-looking QRS complexes, but no pulse can be felt. How is this possible?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The QRS complexes must be interference from the electrodes</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The ECG measures blood flow, so a pulse must be present</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The ECG records electrical activity, which can occur without effective pumping</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The pulse is always delayed by several seconds after each QRS</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"ecg:accc3bcb\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> The ECG shows the electrical signal only. The muscle can fail to pump effectively even while that signal continues, so you must also check the pulse and blood pressure.</p></details></div></section>\n<h2 id=\"3-why-can-ten-electrodes-produce-twelve-ecg-leads\"><span class=\"sec-num\">3.</span> Why can ten electrodes produce twelve ECG leads?</h2>\n<p>A standard <strong>12-lead ECG</strong> uses <strong>10 electrodes</strong>: one on each limb and six on the chest. The machine combines their voltages to give twelve views:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Lead group</th>\n  <th style=\"text-align:right\">How many</th>\n  <th>Names</th>\n  <th>Viewing plane</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td>Standard limb leads (bipolar)</td>\n  <td style=\"text-align:right\">3</td>\n  <td>I, II, III</td>\n  <td>Frontal (front-on)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Augmented limb leads</td>\n  <td style=\"text-align:right\">3</td>\n  <td>aVR, aVL, aVF</td>\n  <td>Frontal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Chest leads</td>\n  <td style=\"text-align:right\">6</td>\n  <td>V1–V6</td>\n  <td>Horizontal (a slice across the chest)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The <strong>right-leg electrode</strong> is not used to make a view. It works as an earth that reduces electrical interference. That is why there are ten electrodes but only nine are used to make the twelve views.</p>\n<h3>The standard limb leads: I, II and III</h3>\n<p>Each one measures the voltage <strong>between two limb electrodes</strong>:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Lead</th>\n  <th>Negative end</th>\n  <th>Positive end</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>I</strong></td>\n  <td>Right arm</td>\n  <td>Left arm</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>II</strong></td>\n  <td>Right arm</td>\n  <td>Left leg</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>III</strong></td>\n  <td>Left arm</td>\n  <td>Left leg</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Together they form a triangle around the heart, <strong>Einthoven's triangle</strong>.</p>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration ecg-figure-small\" id=\"ecg-einthoven\">\n<a href=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/eb/ECG-Einthoven-triangle.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/eb/ECG-Einthoven-triangle.svg\" alt=\"Diagram of Einthoven triangle connecting arm and leg lead positions with bipolar Leads I, II and III.\" width=\"992\" height=\"744\"></a>\n<figcaption><strong>Figure 01. Bipolar limb-lead directions.</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">Kychot · <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ECG-Einthoven-triangle.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikimedia Commons</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY-SA 3.0</a> · unmodified.</span></details></figcaption>\n</figure>\n\n<p>Because the three leads share the same three electrodes, their voltages at any instant are linked by <strong>Einthoven's law</strong>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Einthoven's law</span><span class=\"equation-formula\" role=\"math\" aria-label=\"V subscript II = V subscript I + V subscript III\"><span class=\"eq-layout\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"eq-script\"><span class=\"eq-token eq-identifier\">V</span><sub><span class=\"eq-token eq-identifier\">II</span></sub></span><span class=\"eq-token eq-operator\">=</span><span class=\"eq-script\"><span class=\"eq-token eq-identifier\">V</span><sub><span class=\"eq-token eq-identifier\">I</span></sub></span><span class=\"eq-token eq-operator\">+</span><span class=\"eq-script\"><span class=\"eq-token eq-identifier\">V</span><sub><span class=\"eq-token eq-identifier\">III</span></sub></span></span></span></div>\n\n<p>So if Lead I reads +0.5 mV and Lead III reads +0.3 mV at the same instant, Lead II must read <strong>+0.8 mV</strong>.</p>\n<h3>The augmented limb leads: aVR, aVL and aVF</h3>\n<p>Each uses <strong>one limb electrode as its positive end</strong>, compared with an average of the other two limb electrodes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>aVR</strong> looks from the right arm.</li>\n<li><strong>aVL</strong> looks from the left arm.</li>\n<li><strong>aVF</strong> looks from the left foot.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The lecture calls these leads <strong>unipolar</strong>, because only one electrode forms the positive end. Normal ventricular depolarisation travels away from the right shoulder, so the QRS in <strong>aVR is usually negative</strong>.</p>\n<h3>The chest leads: V1–V6</h3>\n<p>Each chest electrode is a positive end, compared with an average of the limb electrodes. Together they wrap around the heart from right to left.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Electrode</th>\n  <th>Position</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>V1</strong></td>\n  <td>4th intercostal space, right edge of the sternum</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>V2</strong></td>\n  <td>4th intercostal space, left edge of the sternum</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>V3</strong></td>\n  <td>Midway between V2 and V4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>V4</strong></td>\n  <td>5th intercostal space, midclavicular line</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>V5</strong></td>\n  <td>Anterior axillary line, at the level of V4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>V6</strong></td>\n  <td>Midaxillary line, at the level of V4</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Place <strong>V4 before V3</strong>, because V3 sits midway between V2 and V4. A misplaced electrode changes the shapes and can make a normal ECG look abnormal.</p>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration ecg-figure-small\" id=\"ecg-precordial\">\n<a href=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c4/Precordial_Leads_3.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c4/Precordial_Leads_3.svg\" alt=\"Anterior torso and rib cage with six labelled ECG chest electrodes V1 to V6 in their correct anatomical positions.\" width=\"287\" height=\"252\"></a>\n<figcaption><strong>Figure 02. V1–V6 chest electrode positions.</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">Jmarchn · <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Precordial_Leads_3.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikimedia Commons</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY-SA 3.0</a> · unmodified.</span></details></figcaption>\n</figure>\n\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> The lecture names the three lead groups (bipolar, augmented unipolar, chest unipolar). Electrode positions and the right-leg electrode's role are standard clinical teaching.</aside>\n\n<h2 id=\"4-waves-segments-and-intervals-what-is-the-difference\"><span class=\"sec-num\">4.</span> Waves, segments and intervals: what is the difference?</h2>\n<p>The ECG trace is made of three kinds of parts. The words are not interchangeable:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Wave:</strong> a bump up or down from the baseline (P, QRS, T).</li>\n<li><strong>Segment:</strong> the flat stretch <strong>between</strong> two waves (PR segment, ST segment).</li>\n<li><strong>Interval:</strong> a span that <strong>includes at least one wave</strong> (PR interval, QT interval).</li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration ecg-figure-signal\" id=\"ecg-normal-wave\">\n<a href=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9e/SinusRhythmLabels.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9e/SinusRhythmLabels.svg\" alt=\"Labeled normal ECG with P wave, QRS complex, T wave, PR segment and interval, ST segment and QT interval.\" width=\"512\" height=\"512\"></a>\n<figcaption><strong>Figure 03. Normal ECG waves and intervals.</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">Agateller (Anthony Atkielski), SVG conversion by atom · <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SinusRhythmLabels.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikimedia Commons</a> · public domain.</span></details></figcaption>\n</figure>\n\n<div class=\"media-resource video-resource\" data-youtube-id=\"OKJvR83fIrc\" data-media-title=\"(ECG or EKG) Wave patterns. Part 2 — BioVFX\"><span class=\"media-eyebrow\">OPTIONAL VIDEO · BIOVFX ECG SERIES, PART 2 OF 3</span><h3>(ECG or EKG) Wave patterns. Part 2</h3><p>BioVFX. Upward and downward deflections, and how to identify the P, Q, R, S and T waves. It loads only when tapped.</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"media-play\" data-media-action=\"play-video\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Play video</button> <a class=\"media-outlink\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OKJvR83fIrc\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open on YouTube</a><div class=\"media-stage\"></div><p class=\"media-credit\">© BioVFX, shown via YouTube's privacy-enhanced embed. The written explanation on this page does not depend on this video.</p></div>\n\n<p><strong><span data-term=\"p-wave\">P wave</span>: the atria are switched on.</strong> The signal starts in the <span data-term=\"sa-node\">SA node</span> and spreads across both atria. The P wave is that spread through the atrial muscle; the SA node itself is too small to show. The atria contract just after it. Normal duration: <strong>0.08–0.10 s</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>PR interval: from the atria switching on to the ventricles switching on.</strong> It runs from the <strong>start of the P wave</strong> to the <strong>start of the QRS</strong>. Normal: <strong>0.12–0.20 s</strong>. It includes the spread across the atria, the hold-up in the <span data-term=\"av-node\">AV node</span> and the run down the His–<span data-term=\"his-purkinje\">Purkinje fibres</span>. So the PR interval is <strong>not just the AV delay</strong>. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-conduction?section=6-why-is-ventricular-conduction-so-rapid\">→ Cardiac Conduction, §6</a>)</em></p>\n<p>The <strong>PR segment</strong> is shorter. It runs from the <strong>end</strong> of the P wave to the start of the QRS, when the signal is passing through the AV node and His–Purkinje fibres.</p>\n<div class=\"media-resource video-resource\" data-youtube-id=\"MOD3NthLAB0\" data-media-title=\"(ECG or EKG) PR interval part 3 — BioVFX\"><span class=\"media-eyebrow\">OPTIONAL VIDEO · BIOVFX ECG SERIES, PART 3 OF 3</span><h3>(ECG or EKG) PR interval part 3</h3><p>BioVFX. The PR interval, measured from the start of the P wave to the start of the QRS complex, normally about 120–200 ms. It loads only when tapped.</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"media-play\" data-media-action=\"play-video\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Play video</button> <a class=\"media-outlink\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MOD3NthLAB0\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open on YouTube</a><div class=\"media-stage\"></div><p class=\"media-credit\">© BioVFX, shown via YouTube's privacy-enhanced embed. The written explanation on this page does not depend on this video.</p></div>\n\n<p><strong><span data-term=\"qrs\">QRS complex</span>: the ventricles are switched on.</strong> The ventricles have far more muscle than the atria, so the QRS is much bigger than the P wave. Normal duration: <strong>0.06–0.10 s</strong> in the lecture; clinically, anything under 0.12 s is accepted as normal.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q:</strong> a downward deflection before the first upward one</li>\n<li><strong>R:</strong> the upward deflection</li>\n<li><strong>S:</strong> a downward deflection after the R</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Not every lead shows all three.</p>\n<p><strong>Where is the atria resetting?</strong> It happens at the same time as the QRS, and its small signal is hidden inside the much bigger one.</p>\n<p><strong>ST segment: the whole ventricle is switched on.</strong> It runs from the end of the QRS (the <strong>J point</strong>) to the start of the <span data-term=\"t-wave\">T wave</span>. All the ventricular cells are in the plateau of their action potential at the same voltage, so there is no voltage difference for the electrodes to pick up, and the line is flat. <strong>Flat does not mean resting:</strong> this is when the ventricles are contracting hardest. The lecture stresses that the ST segment can rise or fall when the heart muscle is short of oxygen (ischaemia).</p>\n<p><strong>T wave: the ventricles reset.</strong> Resetting spreads more slowly than switching on, so the T wave is longer and lower than the QRS.</p>\n<p><strong>U wave: sometimes seen.</strong> A small wave after the T. The lecture describes it as the last remnants of ventricular repolarisation; its exact source is still debated.</p>\n<p><strong>QT interval: the ventricles switch on and reset.</strong> It runs from the <strong>start of the QRS</strong> to the <strong>end of the T wave</strong>, roughly one ventricular action potential. It is <strong>0.2–0.4 s</strong>, and it <strong>shortens as heart rate rises</strong>, so there is no single normal QT value.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Part</th>\n  <th>What happens</th>\n  <th>Normal (lecture)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>P wave</strong></td>\n  <td>Atria switch on</td>\n  <td>0.08–0.10 s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>PR interval</strong></td>\n  <td>Start of P to start of QRS</td>\n  <td>0.12–0.20 s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>QRS complex</strong></td>\n  <td>Ventricles switch on</td>\n  <td>0.06–0.10 s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>ST segment</strong></td>\n  <td>Whole ventricle switched on (plateau)</td>\n  <td>Flat at the baseline</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>T wave</strong></td>\n  <td>Ventricles reset</td>\n  <td>Longer than QRS</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>QT interval</strong></td>\n  <td>Ventricles switch on and reset</td>\n  <td>0.2–0.4 s, depending on heart rate</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> Values are the lecture's. \"Under 0.12 s\" for the QRS is the usual clinical cut-off. Doctors correct the QT for heart rate (the QTc, often with Bazett's formula, QT ÷ √RR); that is beyond this lecture.</aside>\n\n<h2 id=\"5-connect-the-ecg-to-mechanical-events\"><span class=\"sec-num\">5.</span> Connect the ECG to mechanical events</h2>\n<p>Each electrical event comes <strong>just before</strong> the mechanical event it triggers:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>ECG</th>\n  <th>Electrical event</th>\n  <th>What follows mechanically</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>P wave</strong></td>\n  <td>Atria switch on</td>\n  <td>Atria contract and top up the ventricles</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong><span data-term=\"pr-interval\">PR interval</span></strong></td>\n  <td>The signal is held in the <span data-term=\"av-node\">AV node</span></td>\n  <td>The atria finish emptying before the ventricles contract</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>QRS</strong></td>\n  <td>Ventricles switch on</td>\n  <td>Ventricles contract; <span data-term=\"s1\">S1</span> is heard soon after</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>ST segment</strong></td>\n  <td>Whole ventricle switched on</td>\n  <td>Ventricles eject blood</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>T wave</strong></td>\n  <td>Ventricles reset</td>\n  <td>Ventricles relax; <span data-term=\"s2\">S2</span> comes near the end of the T wave</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The <span data-term=\"wiggers\">Wiggers diagram</span> puts these electrical events above the pressure and volume curves of the same beat. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=9-reading-the-wiggers-diagram-one-trace-at-a-time\">→ Cardiac Cycle, §9</a>)</em></p>\n<section class=\"check-block\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"check-launch\" aria-haspopup=\"dialog\" data-questions=\"4\" data-part=\"Sections 3–5\"><span class=\"cl-badge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">?</span><span class=\"cl-text\"><strong>Understanding Check</strong><span class=\"cl-sub\">Sections 3–5 · 4 questions</span></span><span class=\"cl-go\">Start</span></button><div class=\"check-body\"><p class=\"check-label\"><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 3–5</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> At the same instant, Lead I reads +0.6 mV and Lead III reads +0.4 mV. What does Lead II read?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. +0.2 mV, because Lead II equals Lead I minus Lead III</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. +1.0 mV, because Lead II equals Lead I plus Lead III</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. +0.5 mV, because Lead II is the average of the other two</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. +2.4 mV, because Lead II is the product of the other two</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"ecg:55b914c2\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> Einthoven's law: Lead II = Lead I + Lead III, compared at the same instant.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> A student measures from the end of the P wave to the start of the QRS and calls it the PR interval. What has the student left out?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The QRS complex, which the interval also includes</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The AV delay, which happens after the QRS starts</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The P wave, which the interval includes from its start</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The T wave, which ends the interval</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"ecg:fc98746a\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> The PR interval starts at the <strong>beginning</strong> of the P wave. What the student measured is the PR segment.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> Why is the ST segment flat, even though the ventricles are contracting hard?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The ventricles are electrically silent and fully at rest</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The atria are resetting and cancel out the ventricles</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. All ventricular cells are at the same voltage, so no difference is picked up</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The electrodes stop recording while blood is ejected</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"ecg:3fd6cf54\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> During the plateau, every ventricular cell sits at about the same voltage. With no difference between regions, the lead draws a flat line.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>4.</strong> Why is the QRS complex much bigger than the P wave?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The ventricles have far more muscle to switch on</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The QRS includes the atria resetting as well</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The ventricles are closer to the electrodes</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The AV node amplifies the signal</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"ecg:71a48ad4\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> More muscle switching on gives a bigger total signal. The atria resetting is hidden inside the QRS, but it is tiny and not the reason.</p></details></div></section>\n<h2 id=\"6-how-do-ecg-paper-squares-represent-time-and-voltage\"><span class=\"sec-num\">6.</span> How do ECG paper squares represent time and voltage?</h2>\n<p>ECG paper is a grid of <strong>small squares</strong> (1 mm) grouped into <strong>large squares</strong> (5 small squares, outlined in bold). In the lecture's standard settings, the paper moves at <strong>25 mm/s</strong>, and <strong>10 mm of height = 1 mV</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"ecg-calibration-mini\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"ECG paper calibration\"><div class=\"ecg-paper-sample\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></div><div><strong>At 25 mm/s</strong><p>1 small 1-mm horizontal square = <strong>0.04 s</strong><br>1 large 5-mm horizontal square = <strong>0.20 s</strong></p><p><strong>At 10 mm/mV:</strong> 1 small vertical square = <strong>0.1 mV</strong>; 1 large vertical square = <strong>0.5 mV</strong>.</p></div></div>\n\n<p><strong>Across the paper is time.</strong> The paper moves 25 small squares every second. So:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>1 small square = 1 ÷ 25 = <strong>0.04 s</strong></li>\n<li>1 large square = 5 × 0.04 = <strong>0.20 s</strong></li>\n<li>5 large squares = <strong>1 second</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Up the paper is voltage.</strong> 10 small squares = 1 mV. So:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>1 small square = <strong>0.1 mV</strong></li>\n<li>1 large square = <strong>0.5 mV</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Don't mix the two.</strong> A taller QRS is a bigger voltage, not a longer one. To measure how long something lasts, count squares <strong>across</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Try it:</strong> a PR interval spans 4 small squares. How long is it? 4 × 0.04 = <strong>0.16 s</strong>, which is normal (0.12–0.20 s). In large squares, a normal PR interval is <strong>3 to 5 small squares</strong> (0.12–0.20 s), and a normal QRS is <strong>under 3 small squares</strong> (under 0.12 s).</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-calculate-heart-rate-from-the-tracing\"><span class=\"sec-num\">7.</span> Calculate heart rate from the tracing</h2>\n<p><strong>The idea in one sentence:</strong> work out how long one beat takes, then see how many of those beats fit into a minute.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 1: count the small squares between two R waves.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Step 2: change squares to seconds.</strong> Multiply by 0.04. This is the RR interval, the time for one beat.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 3: change to beats per minute.</strong> Divide 60 by the RR interval.</p>\n<p><strong>The lecture's example: 22 small squares.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Step 1: 22 small squares</li>\n<li>Step 2: 22 × 0.04 = <strong>0.88 s</strong> per beat</li>\n<li>Step 3: 60 ÷ 0.88 ≈ <strong>68 beats/min</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The quicker way, and where 1500 comes from.</strong> In one minute the paper moves 25 mm/s × 60 s = <strong>1500 mm</strong>, which is <strong>1500 small squares</strong>. If one beat takes 22 squares, the number of beats in a minute is:</p>\n<p><strong>1500 ÷ 22 ≈ 68 beats/min.</strong> Same answer, one step.</p>\n<p>Counting large squares works the same way: 1500 small squares = <strong>300 large squares</strong>, so 300 ÷ 4.4 large squares ≈ 68.</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">General relationship</span><span class=\"equation-formula\" role=\"math\" aria-label=\"HR = (60) divided by (RRin seconds)\"><span class=\"eq-layout\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"eq-token eq-identifier\">HR</span><span class=\"eq-token eq-operator\">=</span><span class=\"eq-fraction\"><span class=\"eq-numerator\"><span class=\"eq-token eq-number\">60</span></span><span class=\"eq-denominator\"><span class=\"eq-token eq-identifier\">RR</span><span class=\"eq-token eq-operator\"> </span><span class=\"eq-token eq-words\">in seconds</span></span></span></span></span></div>\n\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">25 mm/s · regular rhythm</span><span class=\"equation-formula\" role=\"math\" aria-label=\"HR = (1500) divided by (N subscript small squares)\"><span class=\"eq-layout\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"eq-token eq-identifier\">HR</span><span class=\"eq-token eq-operator\">=</span><span class=\"eq-fraction\"><span class=\"eq-numerator\"><span class=\"eq-token eq-number\">1500</span></span><span class=\"eq-denominator\"><span class=\"eq-script\"><span class=\"eq-token eq-identifier\">N</span><sub><span class=\"eq-token eq-words\">small squares</span></sub></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"equation-example\">Or 300 ÷ number of large squares between consecutive R waves.</span></div>\n\n<p><strong>No calculator? Use the countdown.</strong> Find an R wave that sits on a thick line. For each thick line after it, say the next number in the countdown. Stop where the next R wave lands:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Next R wave lands after…</th>\n  <th>1 large square</th>\n  <th>2</th>\n  <th>3</th>\n  <th>4</th>\n  <th>5</th>\n  <th>6</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Heart rate (beats/min)</strong></td>\n  <td>300</td>\n  <td>150</td>\n  <td>100</td>\n  <td>75</td>\n  <td>60</td>\n  <td>50</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Each number is just 300 ÷ the number of large squares. If the R wave falls between two lines, the rate lies between those two numbers. For the lecture's strip (4.4 large squares), it lands between 75 and 60, nearer 75: about 68.</p>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure teaching-widget\" data-widget=\"rate-ruler\" data-desc=\"sr\"><figcaption>Rate ruler</figcaption><p>A strip of ECG paper with two R waves. A slider sets the gap between them, from 10 to 40 small squares. Underneath, the three steps update live: squares → seconds (× 0.04) → beats/min (60 ÷ RR), with the 1500 shortcut and the countdown range beside them. It starts at the lecture's 22 squares, 68 beats/min.</p></figure>\n\n<p><strong>A second example:</strong> 15 small squares. 15 × 0.04 = 0.6 s; 60 ÷ 0.6 = <strong>100 beats/min</strong>. Check: 1500 ÷ 15 = 100, or 3 large squares on the countdown = 100. ✓</p>\n<p><strong>If the rhythm is irregular,</strong> the gaps between R waves vary, so one gap gives the rate for one beat only. Instead, count the QRS complexes in a <strong>6-second strip</strong> (30 large squares) and multiply by <strong>10</strong>, because 6 s is one tenth of a minute. Eight complexes in 6 s ≈ <strong>80 beats/min</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Three common mistakes</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Dividing 1500 by large squares.</strong> Use 1500 for small squares and 300 for large squares.</li>\n<li><strong>Using the 1500 rule on an irregular rhythm.</strong> Count complexes in 6 s × 10 instead.</li>\n<li><strong>Forgetting the paper speed.</strong> At 50 mm/s each small square is 0.02 s, so the rules become 3000 ÷ small squares and 600 ÷ large squares.</li>\n</ul>\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> The 1500 and 300 rules and the 22-square example are from the lecture. The countdown, the 6-second method and the 50 mm/s adjustment are standard supplementary teaching.</aside>\n\n<h2 id=\"8-basic-rhythm-assessment-supplementary\"><span class=\"sec-num\">8.</span> Basic rhythm assessment (supplementary)</h2>\n<p>The lecture focuses on ECG basics, not rhythm diagnosis. But four questions turn the trace into a first look at the rhythm:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Rate:</strong> is it between about 60 and 100 beats/min at rest?</li>\n<li><strong>Regularity:</strong> are the RR intervals equal?</li>\n<li><strong>P waves:</strong> is there one before every QRS, and do they all look the same?</li>\n<li><strong>Intervals:</strong> is the PR interval 0.12–0.20 s, and the QRS narrow?</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Normal sinus rhythm</strong> answers yes to all four.</p>\n<p>The PR interval also shows whether the signal is getting through the <span data-term=\"av-node\">AV node</span>:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>What you see</th>\n  <th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td>PR longer than 0.20 s, but every P is followed by a QRS</td>\n  <td><strong>First-degree</strong> <span data-term=\"heart-block\">heart block</span>: delayed, but always gets through</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Some P waves are not followed by a QRS</td>\n  <td><strong>Second-degree</strong> heart block: some signals are dropped</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>P waves and QRS complexes at their own separate rates</td>\n  <td><strong>Third-degree (complete)</strong> heart block: nothing gets through</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The mechanisms are explained in Cardiac Conduction. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-conduction?section=8-clinical-significance-of-conduction-disturbances\">→ Cardiac Conduction, §8</a>)</em></p>\n<section class=\"check-block\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"check-launch\" aria-haspopup=\"dialog\" data-questions=\"5\" data-part=\"Sections 6–8\"><span class=\"cl-badge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">?</span><span class=\"cl-text\"><strong>Understanding Check</strong><span class=\"cl-sub\">Sections 6–8 · 5 questions</span></span><span class=\"cl-go\">Start</span></button><div class=\"check-body\"><p class=\"check-label\"><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 6–8</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> At 25 mm/s, two R waves are 20 small squares apart. What is the heart rate?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. 50 beats/min, because 1000 ÷ 20 = 50</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. 60 beats/min, because 1200 ÷ 20 = 60</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. 75 beats/min, because 1500 ÷ 20 = 75</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. 80 beats/min, because 1600 ÷ 20 = 80</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"ecg:acbbaa5f\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> At 25 mm/s, 1500 small squares pass in a minute, so 1500 ÷ 20 = 75. Check: 20 × 0.04 = 0.8 s, and 60 ÷ 0.8 = 75.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> On a regular strip, each R wave lands 3 large squares after the one before. What is the heart rate?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. About 75 beats/min</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. About 100 beats/min</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. About 150 beats/min</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. About 300 beats/min</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"ecg:db20aa1d\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> The countdown runs 300, 150, 100, so 3 large squares is 100 beats/min (300 ÷ 3).</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> Two QRS complexes look identical, but one is twice as tall. What does the extra height tell you?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. That beat lasted twice as long</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. That beat had a bigger voltage</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. That beat came at twice the rate</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. That beat had a longer PR interval</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"ecg:6d50b43d\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> Height is voltage; width is time. A taller complex is a bigger signal, not a longer one.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>4.</strong> An irregular rhythm shows 9 QRS complexes in a 6-second strip. What is the approximate rate?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. About 54 beats/min, from 9 × 6</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. About 75 beats/min, from 1500 ÷ 20 squares</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. About 90 beats/min, from 9 × 10</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. About 150 beats/min, from 9 ÷ 6 × 100</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"ecg:c752c645\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> A 6-second strip is one tenth of a minute, so multiply by 10. For an irregular rhythm this is more reliable than measuring one RR interval.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>5.</strong> Every P wave is followed by a QRS, but the PR interval is 0.28 s. What does this show?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Complete heart block: no signals get through</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Second-degree heart block: some signals are dropped</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. First-degree heart block: signals are delayed but all get through</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. A normal rhythm, because every P has a QRS</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"ecg:155cc2b3\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> A PR interval over 0.20 s means the signal is slow through the AV node, but since every P is followed by a QRS, none are dropped.</p></details></div></section>\n<h2 id=\"9-chapter-essentials\"><span class=\"sec-num\">9.</span> Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>The ECG records electricity, not pumping.</strong> A normal trace does not prove the heart is ejecting blood.</li>\n<li><strong>Electrode = skin contact; lead = a view.</strong> Ten electrodes give twelve leads; the right-leg electrode is an earth.</li>\n<li><strong>Direction rule:</strong> depolarisation towards a lead draws an upward deflection; away draws a downward one; across draws a small up-and-down one.</li>\n<li><strong>The T wave is usually upright</strong>, because ventricles reset in the reverse order to how they switched on.</li>\n<li><strong>Leads:</strong> I, II and III (bipolar, Einthoven's triangle, II = I + III); aVR, aVL and aVF; V1–V6 across the chest.</li>\n<li><strong>P</strong> = atria switch on (0.08–0.10 s); <strong>QRS</strong> = ventricles switch on (0.06–0.10 s); <strong>T</strong> = ventricles reset.</li>\n<li><strong>PR interval</strong> = start of P to start of QRS (0.12–0.20 s), including the AV delay. The <strong>PR segment</strong> starts at the end of P.</li>\n<li><strong>ST segment</strong> = whole ventricle switched on (plateau): flat but active. It shifts in ischaemia.</li>\n<li><strong>QT interval</strong> = start of QRS to end of T (0.2–0.4 s), shorter at higher heart rates.</li>\n<li><strong>Paper:</strong> 25 mm/s and 10 mm/mV. Small square = 0.04 s and 0.1 mV; large square = 0.20 s.</li>\n<li><strong>Rate (regular rhythm):</strong> 60 ÷ RR (s), 1500 ÷ small squares or 300 ÷ large squares. Irregular: QRS complexes in 6 s × 10.</li>\n<li><strong>Heart block:</strong> first degree = long PR, all conducted; second degree = some P waves dropped; third degree = P and QRS independent.</li>\n</ol>\n";
